## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical thinking on per-tenant privacy, simple policy evaluation, and a lean streaming path. It tests ability to reason about data models, minimal latency, and auditability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policies
- Ingest-time policy evaluation
- Event schema with tenant partitioning
- Data privacy and auditability
- Simple test strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Lightweight policy evaluation skeleton
function evaluatePolicy(event, policyStore) {
  // policyStore: { tenant_id: { redact: [fields] } }
  const p = policyStore[event.tenant_id] || { redact: [] };
  const redacted = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(event.payload || {}));
  p.redact.forEach(f => { if (redacted.hasOwnProperty(f)) redacted[f] = "***"; });
  return { ...event, payload: redacted };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to support dynamic policy updates without downtime?
- What metrics would you monitor to ensure privacy and latency goals?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests privacy-conscious OTCA design, multi-tenant residency, and deletion rights in a real-world, cross-region telemetry pipeline.

## Key Concepts
- PII redaction and tokenization at ingress
- Tenant opt-in/out governance and regional routing
- Immutable audit logs for compliance
- Deletion requests via tombstones with cross-region propagation
- End-to-end testing including opt-out, deletion, and reconciliation

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(event) {
  const e = {...event};
  if (e.payload?.email) e.payload.email = 'REDACTED';
  if (e.payload?.phone) e.payload.phone = 'REDACTED';
  return e;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate no PII leaks in dashboards across regions?
- What metrics would you monitor to detect deletion propagation delays?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-tenant telemetry, privacy, and quota controls for a common data-export workflow.

## Key Concepts
- OTCA telemetry routing in multi-tenant contexts
- Event types around export lifecycles and quotas
- Data sanitization and per-tenant residency
- Schema evolution and dashboard readiness

## Code Example
```javascript
function redactPII(record){
  const r = {...record};
  if(r.user_id) r.user_id = hash(r.user_id);
  if(r.email) delete r.email;
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate per-tenant quota enforcement in bursts?
- What schema changes would you anticipate as features evolve?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical privacy controls in a real OTCA path, focusing on consent-driven data handling and per-tenant residency.

## Key Concepts
- Ingest pipelines with edge gateways and Kafka
- Privacy controls: redact/drop audio data, store digest only
- Per-tenant residency and retention windows

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(event) {
  if (!event.consent_opt_in) delete event.audio_payload;
  event.audio_digest = hash(event.audio_payload || '');
  delete event.audio_payload;
  return event;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test quota enforcement across tenants?
- How would you monitor privacy policy violations in near real time?
